{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by grant heslov, benjira radziwill, and scott neustadter, based on the book the facebook effect by benjamin keating, dennis diker, and michael sayre.\n\nthe film tells the story of mark zuckerberg, a young programmer at harvard university, who creates a website called facebook that would change the way people communicate. the film explores the themes of friendship, loyalty, and the consequences of success.\n\njared leto plays mark zuckerberg, who is portrayed as a brilliant but awkward and lonely college student. the film showcases his struggles to navigate the complexities of relationships and friendships, particularly with his best friend and roommate dylan (armie hammer). however, it is revealed that their friendship is put to the test when mark becomes increasingly obsessed with facebook, leading to tension and conflict.\n\nthe film also focuses on the character of edgar meadow (andrew garfield), a fellow hacker and friend of mark's, who becomes embroiled in the facebook drama. meadow is portrayed as a quiet, introspective individual who is struggling to find his place in the world. his relationship with mark is complex and multifaceted, and the film explores the ways in which their friendship is both supportive and ultimately destructive.\n\nsupporting performances in the film include cassidy freeman as christine \"christy\" pickles, a college student who becomes facebook's first employee, and brian d'arbu (roger fry) as david euler, a rival programmer who seeks to sabotage facebook.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its attention to detail. the filmmakers have clearly done extensive research on the facebook phenomenon, and the film's depiction of the early days of facebook is remarkably accurate. from the cramped, cluttered space where mark and edgar work on facebook to the campus culture of harvard, the film is full of vivid, immersive details that transport the viewer back in time.\n\n(visually, the film is stunning, with a muted color palette and stark, angular lighting that reflects the cold, calculating atmosphere of the facebook headquarters. fincher's direction is masterful, using a combination of close-ups, long takes, and sweeping montages to build tension and create a sense of urgency.)\n\nthe film's score, composed by Trent Reznor and atticus ross, is equally impressive, capturing the mood and atmosphere of each scene with precision and nuance. the soundtrack is a key element in setting the tone of the film, and it perfectly complements the visuals and performances.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping, thought-provoking film that explores the complexities of friendship, ambition, and the consequences of success. it is a film that will resonate with anyone who has ever experienced the thrill of creating something new, or the pain of losing something precious.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.20552276074886322}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.00035589560866355896}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.04680097848176956}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0009609610424377024}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.5923129320144653}, {"token": "201", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " american", "logprob": -0.2819807529449463}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -0.016800811514258385}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -1.847726889536716e-05}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.0005004345439374447}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -9.393251093570143e-05}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.09783100336790085}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.0009151084814220667}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.044229116290807724}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -1.5735502529423684e-05}